const body = `<h1>4.5b &#8212; Pattern Printer (Build Together)</h1>

<p>
    <strong>Type:</strong> Basic Application. The teacher builds, you follow along.
    Nested loops turn abstract loop tracing into visible output. You'll see
    exactly what an inner loop does relative to its outer loop.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Basic Application</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>The Goal</h3>

    <p>Print a right triangle of stars using nested for loops.</p>

    <pre><code><span class="output">*
**
***
****
*****</span></code></pre>

    <h3>Iteration 1: Single Row First</h3>

    <p>Before nesting, make sure you can print a single row.
    Create <code>PatternPrinter.java</code>:</p>

    <pre><code>public class PatternPrinter {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Print 5 stars on one line
        for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
            System.out.print("*");
        }
        System.out.println(); // move to next line
    }
}</code></pre>

    <p>Run it. You should see <code>*****</code> on one line.</p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic Checkpoint</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>What's the difference between <code>print</code> and
                <code>println</code> here? Why do we need both?</li>
            <li>If you changed the loop to <code>i < 3</code>, how
                many stars would print?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <h3>Iteration 2: Repeat the Row (Broken!)</h3>

    <p>Wrap the existing code in another for loop. Let's see what
    happens:</p>

    <pre><code>for (int row = 0; row < 5; row++) {
    for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
        System.out.print("*");
    }
    System.out.println();
}</code></pre>

    <p>Run it. Is the result what you expected? Why or why not?</p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt</h3>
        <p>The condition inside the inner loop currently says <code>i &lt; 5</code> —
        the same thing for every row.</p>
        <p><strong>What needs to change in the inner loop's condition to make
        the rows grow instead of staying the same length?</strong></p>
    </div>

    <h3>Iteration 3: The Inverted Triangle Trap</h3>

    <p>Change the inner loop's condition to <code>i < row</code>.
    Run it. You get:</p>

    <pre><code>
*
**
***
****</code></pre>

    <p>Almost right, but row 0 printed 0 stars. Why?</p>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker Challenge</h3>
        <p>Fix it so row 0 prints 1 star and row 4 prints 5 stars.
        There's more than one way to do it — find one, and be ready to
        explain why it works.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Iteration 4: Different Patterns</h3>

    <p>Now that you understand how the outer row variable controls
    the inner loop, try these patterns. For each one, write pseudocode
    BEFORE you write Java:</p>

    <pre><code>*****          *               *****
****           **              ****
***            ***             ***
**             ****            **
*              *****           *</code></pre>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic Checkpoint</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>For the middle triangle (spaces before stars): how
                many spaces do you need on each row? What's the
                relationship between the row number and the number
                of spaces?</li>
            <li>Why does nested loop tracing matter for your RPG?
                Think about your TileMap: what two things are you
                looping over to draw every tile?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

